Context (pp. 41-50). In this 'third version' of Analysis situs, Grothendieck is trying to axiomatize a tame, combinatorial topology as an order-theoretic structure. The data are (M, ≤, ≪ or ⋘, ≭) together with a distinguished subset Λ ⊂ M, governed by axioms MΛ1-MΛ5 (stated around p. 41). The intended model is a family of 'strata' or pieces of a space:
- ≤ is inclusion;
- X' ⋘ X means 'well inside' (relatively compact in, or closure contained in);
- X̃ is the set of elements of M below X;
- omb_Λ(X), the 'shadow' of X, is the set of Λ-elements well inside X; for a subset P, omb_Λ(P) is the union of the shadows of its elements;
- ∥ is disjointness. On Λ it is primitive. On M it is defined by X ∥_M Y iff every shadow element of X is disjoint from every shadow element of Y;
- X ≭ Y is a 'non-meeting / transversality' type relation defined on p. 48.

Previous page. Lemma 1(a) says X ∥ Y iff X ≭ Y and L := X̃ ∩ Ỹ = ∅. This is the case where the 'intersection' L is empty.

This page tries to localize Lemma 1 to the case L ≠ ∅.

First conjectural lemma. Take X, Y ∈ M with X ≭ Y, put L = X̃ ∩ Ỹ, and take X' ⋘ X, Y' ⋘ Y. Set X'_L = X̃' ∩ Omb(L) and Y'_L = Ỹ' ∩ Omb(L), the parts of X' and Y' lying in the shadow of the common part. The claim is X'_L ∥ Y'_L ⟺ X' ∥ Y'. In words: two well-inside pieces can only meet inside the shadow of the intersection of the ambient pieces. One direction (the one from X' ∥ Y') is trivial. Grothendieck finds the other plausible but cannot derive it from MΛ5 alone. He thinks MΛ5 must be replaced by a stronger, 'somewhat discouraging' form.

He then notes that this lemma relates to MΛ4 of p. 41, and that what must be proved is MΛ5 of that page, in the form of a second conjectural lemma. For X ≭ Y and L = X̃ ∩ Ỹ, it states omb_Λ(X) ∩ omb_Λ(Y) = omb_Λ(L). That is, the shadow map converts the 'intersection' of X and Y into the intersection of shadows; shadow commutes with meets.

He cannot prove this either. He concludes it would need a new axiom MΛ6, tying together two conditions on ∥ (the relation ∥_Λ extended via the ≭ defined on p. 48). He then worries about the many consequences, and about whether the propositions of p. 42 would still hold 'topologically' for structures (M, ≤, ≭, ≭, Λ) arising from concrete models satisfying MΛ1, MΛ2. He ends: 'Rien de concret! On oublie!' and abandons this line. On p. 50 he restarts with a recapitulation and the idea of an independent 'algebra of strata / multistrata'.

Purpose: to build an axiomatic, point-free theory of 'spacieux / polyédral' objects (tame topology, cf. Esquisse §5), in which intersections of strata are controlled purely order-theoretically.
